    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks, that makes sense. My concern is that these aren't just single errors; they seem to be happening almost \"randomly\" across different departments. Like, we'll see a small amount missing in one week, then nothing for three, then a slightly different amount in another department. It feels less like a single person making a mistake and more like these little amounts are just... popping up unexpectedly in the data. \n\nHow would you recommend I track if there's a specific pattern to the timing or the specific items being 'miscellaneous' so I can pin down where the leak is coming from?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To track a hidden pattern across departments, employ temporal and categorical clustering on the data. Graph the instances of these miscellaneous entries over time to see if they correlate with specific operational events, such as inventory receiving days or shift changes. Next, categorize the reported items further—even generically (e.g., \"writing instruments,\" \"cleaning aids\")—to look for a recurring item type across different locations. This combination of time-series and categorical analysis can reveal a common trigger or consistent type of misuse.",
